"A Kubernetes ownership diagram distinguishes resource owner references, selectors, and reconciliation responsibilities.\n\nUse when: Explain how workload controllers create and replace resources. Review deletion propagation and resource ownership.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is the path a client request takes through Services.\n\nVisual grammar: Typed nodes represent Kubernetes API objects. Owner-reference edges differ from selector and reference edges. Annotations identify controller ownership and relevant deletion behavior.\n\nBuild: Choose a namespace, cluster scope, and resource snapshot. Draw owner-reference chains from actual metadata or mark them proposed. Add selectors or referenced objects using separately labeled edge styles. Lay out owners above dependents and route nonownership links through side corridors.\n\nCheck: Owner references are not confused with label selection. Namespaced ownership respects Kubernetes scope constraints. Garbage-collection claims match propagation and finalizer behavior. Resource kind labels and edge-type annotations remain unobstructed.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a Service owns the Pods it selects. Inferring ownership solely from similar names or labels.\n\nExample: A Deployment owns a ReplicaSet, which owns Pods; a Service selects matching Pods through a distinctly labeled relationship.\n\nAlternatives: Kubernetes traffic-flow diagram, Deployment diagram."
